package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part story about the bridge of blue tiles. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch45 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_bridge_of_blue_tiles",
            title = "The Bridge of Blue Tiles",
            tagline = "A school art club follows a pattern of handmade tiles to uncover the people who built a beloved footbridge.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "A Colour Missing", listOf(
                    "The footbridge over the Omi stream had a blue-tile border that residents used as a landmark. During a school photography project, Lami noticed that one section was plain concrete. The council's maintenance notice said the tiles had been removed after storm damage, but no replacement design was listed. She asked her art teacher whether the club could propose a respectful restoration.",
                    "Mr James said the bridge was public property, so the students would need permission and advice from the council. He encouraged them to research its history first. At the library, Lami found a photograph showing the bridge at its opening. The blue border contained repeating shapes: a wave, a leaf, and a small square. A caption credited 'the riverside workshop' without naming its members."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Workshop", listOf(
                    "The librarian found a community newsletter from the year the bridge opened. It described a group of ceramic artists who had made tiles from local clay. Their workshop had been located near the market, but the building was now a tailoring shop. Lami and her classmate Seyi visited with their teacher and asked the shop owner whether any records remained.",
                    "The owner remembered a cupboard of old papers left by the previous tenant. With permission, they found a folder containing sketches and a list of workshop members. The pattern was not random: the wave represented the stream, the leaf represented the riverside trees, and the square represented homes along the path. The students copied the designs for research, not for construction, and asked the council for the original specifications."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Difficult Measurement", listOf(
                    "The council engineer explained that the bridge surface had to remain safe and accessible. Any new tiles would need to meet current standards for grip, durability, and maintenance. The old photographs were useful for history, but they could not be treated as a building plan. Lami was disappointed that the club could not simply replace the missing section with handmade tiles.",
                    "The engineer suggested a separate art panel near the bridge entrance, away from the walking surface. It could tell the story without affecting the structure. The students agreed to develop a proposal and include the workshop artists if they could locate them. Seyi noticed that one name in the folder appeared beside a small star: A. Okorie, design coordinator."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Designer's Sketchbook", listOf(
                    "An alumni directory connected A. Okorie to a retired art teacher, Mrs Okorie, who lived nearby. She welcomed the students and showed them a sketchbook from the workshop. The original designs had been made collaboratively, with each artist contributing a motif. Mrs Okorie said the bridge had been a community project, not the work of one celebrated designer.",
                    "She also explained that the workshop had closed when its members moved for jobs and family responsibilities. The tiles were donated, and the council had installed them with professional help. Mrs Okorie was glad the students wanted to remember everyone, but she asked them not to copy the old tiles without permission. The club added a consent and credit plan to its proposal."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Names in the Margin", listOf(
                    "The sketchbook contained a list of contributors, but some names were initials. Mrs Okorie recognised several and helped the archivist find public records and former workshop announcements. The club created a timeline that clearly separated confirmed facts from memories. They did not publish private addresses or contact details.",
                    "At the next meeting, a former member named Mr Adeyemi joined by phone. He remembered making the leaf pattern and said another artist, Binta, had designed the wave. He offered a photograph of the group, provided everyone pictured agreed to its use. Mrs Okorie contacted the others through a former members' association, and the club waited for permission before including the image."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A New Design", listOf(
                    "The art club presented three ideas for the interpretation panel. One used a simple timeline, another showed the three symbols, and the third combined both with a space for the artists' names. The council's heritage officer preferred the third because visitors could understand the design and learn who made it. The engineer confirmed that the panel could be installed on an existing notice plinth.",
                    "The students revised the layout with larger lettering, strong contrast, and a height suitable for seated visitors. They included a short note that the bridge itself had been inspected and repaired by qualified staff. The panel would not suggest that visitors should touch or climb the structure. Lami realised that good public art had to consider the people who would encounter it, not just the people who designed it."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Artists Return", listOf(
                    "Several former workshop members attended a planning session at the library. They corrected the spelling of names and explained how the patterns had been chosen. Binta said the wave had been inspired by the stream after rain, while Mr Adeyemi remembered collecting leaf shapes during supervised walks with the group. Their memories differed on small details, so the archivist recorded each account as an individual recollection.",
                    "The artists agreed to the panel and approved the use of the group photograph. They asked that the credit list say 'Riverside Tile Workshop' and name each confirmed contributor. The council agreed to fund the panel from its community heritage budget. The missing tiles would remain absent until a separate engineering decision was made; the art project would not interfere with bridge safety."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Panel Takes Shape", listOf(
                    "A local sign maker produced a weather-resistant proof. The students checked every name against the artists' corrections and the archive record. Lami spotted that the leaf symbol had been printed upside down. The sign maker corrected it and sent a new proof. Seyi tested the text from several distances to make sure the words were readable.",
                    "The council's accessibility adviser reviewed the final version and recommended a clearer route to the panel, without narrowing the footpath. The installation team handled the work and confirmed that the plinth was stable. The art club watched from the designated area, taking photographs for its school report. The bridge remained open under the council's normal safety arrangements."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "Opening Day", listOf(
                    "At the opening, the former artists stood beside the panel while the heritage officer described the project. The group photograph showed them much younger, holding tiles in a workshop filled with sunlight. Binta spoke about how each person had contributed one small idea. She said the bridge had become meaningful because people could recognise something of their neighbourhood in its design.",
                    "Lami presented the students' research folder to the library. It included sources, permissions, corrections, and a note explaining which parts of the story were based on personal memories. The council thanked the club for not rushing into physical repairs. The students had helped restore the bridge's history while leaving engineering decisions to the people responsible for them."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Pattern Continues", listOf(
                    "Months later, the panel had become a small meeting point. Residents stopped to read the names, and a local school asked the artists to speak about collaborative design. The council separately assessed the bridge surface and scheduled maintenance through its normal process. Nobody promised that the missing tiles would return, but the history was no longer hidden.",
                    "Lami added a final photograph to the club's archive: the blue patterns in the old picture beside the new panel. The bridge still crossed the stream, carrying people safely between two neighbourhoods. Its story now included the artists, the engineers, the residents, and the students who had asked a simple question about an empty space. The missing colour had led them to a fuller picture, and the bridge's shared history would remain visible."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
